          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#general General
          %p
            %em Quantum error correction
            is concerned with ensuring the robustness of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            against
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noise"} noise
            (as in classical
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/error+correction"} error correction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+noise"} quantum noise
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+decoherence"} quantum decoherence
            (e.g. against
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bit+flip+channel"} bit flip errors
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#FerrisPoulin13"} Ferris &amp; Poulin 2013
          %blockquote
            %p
              The basic principle of quantum error correction (QEC) is to encode information into the long-range correlations of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+entanglement"} entangled
              quantum many-body states in such a way that it cannot be accessed locally. When a local error affects the system, it leaves a detectable imprint—called the error syndrome. The decoding problem consists in inferring the recovery with greatest probability of success given the error syndrome. In general, this is a hard problem, but for well-chosen codes, it can be solved efficiently either exactly or heuristically.
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Steane06"} Steane 06
          %blockquote
            %p Error correction is especially important in quantum computers, because efficient quantum algorithms make use of large scale quantum interference, which is fragile, i.e. sensitive to imprecision in the computer and to unwanted coupling between the computer and the rest of the world. This makes large scale quantum computation so difficult as to be practically impossible unless error correction methods are used. Indeed, before quantum error correction was discovered, it seemed that this fragility would forbid large scale quantum computation. The ability to correct a quantum computer efficiently without disturbing the coherence of the computation is highly non-intuitive, and was thought more or less impossible. The discovery of powerful error correction methods therefore caused much excitement, since it converted large scale quantum computation from a practical impossibility to a possibility.
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#DevittNemotoMunro09"} Devitt, Nemoto &amp; Munro 2009
          %blockquote
            %p
              Starting in 1995, several papers appeared, in rapid succession, proposing codes which were appropriate to perform error correction on quantum data (
              %a{:href => "#Shor95"}> Sho95
              ;
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "#Steane96"} Ste96a
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "#CalderbankShor96"} CS96
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "#LMPZ96"} LMPZ96
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#BDSW96"} BSW96
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#ZhuCross20"} Zhu &amp; Cross 20
          %blockquote
            %p Although we are currently in an era of quantum computers with tens of noisy qubits, it is likely that a decisive, practical quantum advantage can only be achieved with a scalable, fault-tolerant, error-corrected quantum computer. Therefore, development of quantum error correction is one of the central themes of the next five to ten years.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Lucero21"}> Lucero 21
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Within the decade, Google aims to build a useful, error-corrected quantum computer.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\cdots]
              Our journey to build an error-corrected quantum computer within the decade includes several scientific milestones, including building an error-corrected logical qubit.
          %p
            Specifically on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+tensor+network"} holographic quantum error correcting codes
            (see references
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesViaHolographicTensorNetworks"} below
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Harlow20"} Harlow 20
          %blockquote
            %p The codes provided by AdS/CFT often come close to saturating theoretical bounds on the performance of quantum codes. It seems AdS/CFT may be a tool for discovering better quantum cryptography?
          %p
            %a{:href => "#WVSB20"}> WVSB 20
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+tensor+network"} holographic codes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              could be promising candidates to circumvent our results and could possibly realise a universal set of unitary implementations of logical operators.
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#CDCW21"} CDCW 21
          %blockquote
            %p
              There are a number of reasons to suspect that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+tensor+network"} holographic codes
              may be of practical use for quantum computing.
            %p Holographic codes can admit erasure thresholds comparable to that of the widely-studied surface code, and likewise for their threshold against Pauli errors. Their holographic structure also naturally leads to an organization of encoded qubits into a hierarchy of levels of protection from errors, which could be useful for applications which call for many qubits withvarying levels of protection. In particular, this is reminiscent of many schemes for magic state distillation – and indeed, the concatenated codes utilized for magic state distillation share a similar hierarchical structure to holographic codes. The layered structure of holographic codes is also reminiscent of memory architectures in classical computers, where it is useful to have different levels of short- and long-term memory. Although these codes have some notable drawbacks, in particular holographic stabilizer codes require nonlocal stabilizer generators, other codes such as concatenated codes suffer similar drawbacks and have still proven to be useful. Conversely, the stringent requirement of non-local stabilizer generators allows holographic codes to protect many more qubits than a topological code and in fact attain a finite nonzero encoding rate, which is typically not possible for topological codes. Nonetheless, many open questions remain about the usefulness of holographic codes for fault-tolerant quantum computing.
          %h3#quantum_error_correcting_codes Quantum error correcting codes
          %p
            The simple but important special case of passive correction of erasures may be handled by
            = succeed ":" do
              %em quantum error correcting codes
          %p
            Recall that a classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/error+correcting+code"} error correcting code
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            of states
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is, typically, a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injection
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            into some larger set, typically a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+power"} Cartesian power
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi code
                  %mi P
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi H
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ×
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L \overset{code}{\hookrightarrow} P \coloneqq H \times \cdots \times H
          %p
            (often considered in the form of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+codes"} linear codes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            of sets of states is replaced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℒ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}
            a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional
            in practice), a
            %em quantum error correcting code
            is a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+subspace"} linear embedding
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %em code subspace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℒ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}
            into a larger Hilbert spaces, often an
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            of copies of some
            = succeed ":" do
